1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,810
Hello everyone, this is Ria!

2
00:00:03,810 --> 00:00:12,420
Today, I decorated a bit with a green, fresh, natural vibe.

3
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,800
Kind of a nature feeling?

4
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:21,990
Today, we’re going to talk about mountains! Mountains.

5
00:00:21,990 --> 00:00:23,050
Mountains.

6
00:00:23,050 --> 00:00:26,140
Do you like mountains?

7
00:00:26,140 --> 00:00:31,780
What do you call climbing a mountain in Korean?

8
00:00:31,780 --> 00:00:33,060
Anyone know?

9
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:35,170
Anyone know?

10
00:00:35,170 --> 00:00:38,590
Yes, it’s called 등산 (hiking/mountain climbing).

11
00:00:38,590 --> 00:00:40,050
등산

12
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:41,950
It’s a Sino-Korean word.

13
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:50,130
등 means to go up, and 산 means mountain, so together it’s 등산.

14
00:00:50,130 --> 00:00:55,070
Do you like hiking or mountain climbing?

15
00:00:55,070 --> 00:00:59,810
I uploaded a video before.

16
00:00:59,810 --> 00:01:05,670
It was a scene looking out the window inside an airplane.

17
00:01:05,670 --> 00:01:14,170
Looking outside the window, I could see mountains and the sea below.

18
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:20,060
While showing that, I asked you a question.

19
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:25,940
Do you like mountains? Or do you like the sea?

20
00:01:25,940 --> 00:01:35,600
Honestly, I thought almost everyone would say the sea.

21
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:43,550
I thought most people would naturally prefer the sea.

22
00:01:43,550 --> 00:01:46,010
That was my misunderstanding.

23
00:01:46,010 --> 00:01:47,200
Misunderstanding.

24
00:01:47,200 --> 00:01:52,380
Many people actually like mountains too.

25
00:01:52,380 --> 00:01:57,560
Some said they like the sea in summer and the mountains in winter!

26
00:01:57,560 --> 00:02:00,970
Someone said that.

27
00:02:00,970 --> 00:02:03,790
It depends on the season.

28
00:02:03,790 --> 00:02:07,710
Someone said that too.

29
00:02:07,710 --> 00:02:14,000
But me... do I like mountains?

30
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:19,440
Personally, I don’t really like mountains.

31
00:02:19,440 --> 00:02:21,140
Personally!

32
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:25,330
Personally, I don’t really like mountains.

33
00:02:25,330 --> 00:02:29,110
I don’t really like hiking.

34
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:31,130
There’s a reason.

35
00:02:31,130 --> 00:02:35,260
This is kind of my embarrassing past.

36
00:02:35,260 --> 00:02:36,830
Embarrassing past.

37
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:43,290
A long time ago, we all went to a mountain from school.

38
00:02:43,290 --> 00:02:49,260
I went to a mountain with my classmates.

39
00:02:49,260 --> 00:02:56,730
Honestly, I didn’t know the mountain was that high.

40
00:02:56,730 --> 00:03:04,780
Not knowing it was high, I just wore any shoes.

41
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:07,760
Really any shoes.

42
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:10,180
Shoes like slippers...

43
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:13,750
I wore shoes like slippers.

44
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:17,860
Going up the mountain was okay.

45
00:03:17,860 --> 00:03:23,360
I had fun chatting with the kids while climbing.

46
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:28,900
And finally, we reached the summit.

47
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:30,210
Summit.

48
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:38,800
The highest point on a mountain is called 정상 (summit) in Korean.

49
00:03:38,800 --> 00:03:47,350
Suddenly I remembered, what do you say when you reach the summit?

50
00:03:47,350 --> 00:03:52,310
In Korean, you shout “Yaho!”

51
00:03:52,310 --> 00:03:53,830
Yaho!

52
00:03:53,830 --> 00:03:59,750
When you shout like that, the sound echoes back.

53
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:01,990
The sound echoes back.

54
00:04:01,990 --> 00:04:05,990
What do you call that in Korean?

55
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:08,700
It’s called 메아리 (echo).

56
00:04:08,700 --> 00:04:10,040
메아리

57
00:04:10,040 --> 00:04:16,460
If you say “Yaho,” you hear “Yaho” back from far away, right?

58
00:04:16,460 --> 00:04:21,780
What do you say when you climb a mountain?

59
00:04:21,780 --> 00:04:25,410
What do you say in your language?

60
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:30,210
And have you ever heard an echo?

61
00:04:30,210 --> 00:04:33,940
Anyway, I was talking about my embarrassing past, right?

62
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:40,060
At that time, I wore sandals-like shoes for hiking.

63
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:47,930
We reached the summit, and when going down, the problems started.

64
00:04:47,930 --> 00:04:50,950
The mountain has a slope.

65
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:52,200
Slope.

66
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:58,140
Slope means how much the ground is tilted.

67
00:04:58,140 --> 00:05:05,140
When going down, the slope of the mountain was very steep.

68
00:05:05,140 --> 00:05:07,320
What does steep mean?

69
00:05:07,320 --> 00:05:11,160
It means the slope is severe.

70
00:05:11,160 --> 00:05:17,940
Going down was very hard because the slope was steep.

71
00:05:17,940 --> 00:05:21,430
I was crazy!

72
00:05:21,430 --> 00:05:27,530
I was crazy for not wearing proper shoes.

73
00:05:27,530 --> 00:05:32,570
I think I underestimated the mountain.

74
00:05:32,570 --> 00:05:40,440
To underestimate means to think something is easier than it really is.

75
00:05:40,440 --> 00:05:42,580
“Oh? This looks easy?”

76
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:44,790
“I think it’ll be okay?”

77
00:05:44,790 --> 00:05:46,070
“This much is nothing...”

78
00:05:46,070 --> 00:05:49,940
That’s what underestimating means.

79
00:05:49,940 --> 00:05:57,460
I was punished for underestimating the local mountain.

80
00:05:57,460 --> 00:06:00,420
I got punished.

81
00:06:00,420 --> 00:06:05,620
I kept falling while going down.

82
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:09,020
At first, it was just funny.

83
00:06:09,020 --> 00:06:14,360
Also, when you’re young, everyone laughs a lot.

84
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:22,140
I laughed, my friends laughed, the situation was really fun.

85
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:33,020
But as I went down the mountain, I kept falling more and more.

86
00:06:33,020 --> 00:06:38,870
So the mood... got worse and worse.

87
00:06:38,870 --> 00:06:44,890
I was hurt and my friends started to worry.

88
00:06:44,890 --> 00:06:52,530
Above all, our teacher was very worried.

89
00:06:52,530 --> 00:06:57,850
In Korean schools, there are several classes.

90
00:06:57,850 --> 00:07:00,280
Class.

91
00:07:00,280 --> 00:07:11,250
There is a teacher in charge of one class, and that person is called the homeroom teacher.

92
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:13,390
Homeroom teacher.

93
00:07:13,390 --> 00:07:19,320
Or more casually, we call them 담임쌤 (homeroom teacher, informal).

94
00:07:19,320 --> 00:07:24,730
But this homeroom teacher started to worry.

95
00:07:24,730 --> 00:07:31,900
Because the homeroom teacher is responsible for the students in the class.

96
00:07:31,900 --> 00:07:40,680
So the teacher said, “What should we do!... This won’t do... Can someone help support her?” and

97
00:07:40,680 --> 00:07:45,560
one of the classmates helped support me.

98
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:54,040
To support means to help someone walk by their side.

99
00:07:54,040 --> 00:08:03,260
Usually, when someone is physically uncomfortable or having a hard time, you support them.

100
00:08:03,260 --> 00:08:04,490
Support.

101
00:08:04,490 --> 00:08:11,890
Especially legs! When legs are uncomfortable, this word is often used.

102
00:08:11,890 --> 00:08:15,080
Shall I support you a bit?

103
00:08:15,080 --> 00:08:17,180
Shall I support you?

104
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:20,050
You can say like this.

105
00:08:20,050 --> 00:08:30,620
So until we finished going down the mountain, I almost leaned on that friend to come down.

106
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:33,920
I leaned on them to come down.

107
00:08:33,920 --> 00:08:35,840
It was so embarrassing.

108
00:08:35,840 --> 00:08:38,640
Really embarrassing.

109
00:08:38,640 --> 00:08:49,220
Anyway, because of this not-so-good memory from school days, I don’t really like mountains.

110
00:08:49,220 --> 00:08:52,950
I don’t like them that much.

111
00:08:52,950 --> 00:09:02,930
But very occasionally, when the weather is nice, I do go hiking with my family.

112
00:09:02,930 --> 00:09:07,510
I looked it up on the internet.

113
00:09:07,510 --> 00:09:09,000
I looked it up.

114
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:15,670
But there are some characteristics of Korean hikers, everyone!

115
00:09:15,670 --> 00:09:17,720
Hikers.

116
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:26,250
When hiking, a characteristic of Korean people is... first, there are many elderly people.

117
00:09:26,250 --> 00:09:29,300
Older seniors.

118
00:09:29,300 --> 00:09:34,230
And their clothing style!

119
00:09:34,230 --> 00:09:42,420
How they dress, their style is very interesting.

120
00:09:42,420 --> 00:09:55,770
I’m used to seeing this, but foreigners find it very interesting.

121
00:09:55,770 --> 00:10:03,290
Very colorful clothes, bags, hiking gear.

122
00:10:03,290 --> 00:10:04,600
Gear.

123
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:12,180
They carry a lot of these and prepare properly for hiking.

124
00:10:12,180 --> 00:10:28,390
Even though it’s not a very high mountain, not like Everest, every time they hike, they wear these colorful hiking clothes.

125
00:10:28,390 --> 00:10:32,260
That looks very interesting.

126
00:10:32,260 --> 00:10:34,310
What do you think, everyone?

127
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:37,620
What do you think?

128
00:10:37,620 --> 00:10:43,880
I have a lot of experience falling on mountains.

129
00:10:43,880 --> 00:10:51,890
So I understand why Korean seniors carry so much gear,

130
00:10:51,890 --> 00:10:59,820
and why they prepare properly.

131
00:10:59,820 --> 00:11:07,090
As people get older, they have to be more careful about accidents.

132
00:11:07,090 --> 00:11:08,240
Accidents.

133
00:11:08,240 --> 00:11:16,300
Especially when you get older, you never know when or where something might happen.

134
00:11:16,300 --> 00:11:19,370
So you have to be careful.

135
00:11:19,370 --> 00:11:24,280
I totally understand.

136
00:11:24,280 --> 00:11:31,190
But foreigners seem to dress more freely.

137
00:11:31,190 --> 00:11:36,470
Just wearing a tank top and shorts...

138
00:11:36,470 --> 00:11:49,870
When these very colorfully dressed Korean seniors meet foreigners dressed simply like this, wouldn’t it be funny?

139
00:11:49,870 --> 00:11:52,790
A situation where they don’t understand each other?

140
00:11:52,790 --> 00:11:57,260
“Hmm? What’s this?” kind of feeling.

141
00:11:57,260 --> 00:12:03,750
Today, I want to talk a bit about my hiking experiences.

142
00:12:03,750 --> 00:12:16,470
I want to talk about hiking with you, but as I said earlier, I don’t really like hiking.

143
00:12:16,470 --> 00:12:22,070
So nothing special comes to mind.

144
00:12:22,070 --> 00:12:28,800
Um... but I do like trekking!

145
00:12:28,800 --> 00:12:33,240
Trekking is a bit better, I think.

146
00:12:33,240 --> 00:12:44,970
Trekking doesn’t necessarily mean going to the mountain summit; it’s more like walking on mountain trails.

147
00:12:44,970 --> 00:12:53,030
I like walking on the trail and looking at the surrounding scenery.

148
00:12:53,030 --> 00:13:00,120
The first place I tried trekking was Australia.

149
00:13:00,120 --> 00:13:04,630
There’s a place in Australia called Burleigh Heads.

150
00:13:04,630 --> 00:13:08,220
Wow, this place was amazing, everyone!

151
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:11,820
It’s a trip of a lifetime for me.

152
00:13:11,820 --> 00:13:14,240
Trip of a lifetime.

153
00:13:14,240 --> 00:13:21,820
I didn’t expect it to be this beautiful, but trekking there was so great.

154
00:13:21,820 --> 00:13:28,520
When you go up a little, you can see the sea.

155
00:13:28,520 --> 00:13:30,260
Wow, amazing!

156
00:13:30,260 --> 00:13:36,760
The weather was perfect, and I could hear the sound of waves from the sea.

157
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:41,020
All of this was just so wonderful.

158
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:49,140
When we go swimming, we see the sea up close.

159
00:13:49,140 --> 00:13:52,780
We play near the sea.

160
00:13:52,780 --> 00:14:02,810
But when trekking, seeing the sea from above had a different charm.

161
00:14:02,810 --> 00:14:08,890
It had a different charm than seeing the sea up close.

162
00:14:08,890 --> 00:14:15,260
If I find photos, I’ll upload them here.

163
00:14:15,260 --> 00:14:23,730
Walking while looking at the sea, then you come to a forest.

164
00:14:23,730 --> 00:14:32,540
A forest is a place with many trees and mountains.

165
00:14:32,540 --> 00:14:38,550
There were many interesting flowers in that forest.

166
00:14:38,550 --> 00:14:42,570
The most interesting thing was a lizard!

167
00:14:42,570 --> 00:14:47,370
I saw a really big lizard there.

168
00:14:47,370 --> 00:14:55,690
But that lizard’s tail... the tail was gone.

169
00:14:55,690 --> 00:14:58,840
The tail was cut off.

170
00:14:58,840 --> 00:15:09,820
Wow, it was the first time in my life seeing such a big lizard... and it had a cut tail.

171
00:15:09,820 --> 00:15:12,760
Wow, this is Australia after all.

172
00:15:12,760 --> 00:15:15,740
This is the nature of Australia.

173
00:15:15,740 --> 00:15:18,610
That’s what I thought.

174
00:15:18,610 --> 00:15:26,630
Walking through the forest, I saw something really amazing.

175
00:15:26,630 --> 00:15:33,050
Suddenly, I saw something between the trees.

176
00:15:33,050 --> 00:15:35,060
I saw something.

177
00:15:35,060 --> 00:15:42,100
So I went down there... wow... amazing!

178
00:15:42,100 --> 00:15:45,990
There was a very beautiful waterfront.

179
00:15:45,990 --> 00:15:47,200
Waterfront.

180
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:55,000
It wasn’t the sea, but water gently rippling like this.

181
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,030
Rippling.

182
00:15:57,030 --> 00:16:04,340
This means the sound or sight of water lightly shaking.

183
00:16:04,340 --> 00:16:08,120
It has to feel light.

184
00:16:08,120 --> 00:16:15,060
Not the strong waves crashing in the sea, but a light feeling!

185
00:16:15,060 --> 00:16:17,150
Rippling.

186
00:16:17,150 --> 00:16:20,860
The water color was also very pretty.

187
00:16:20,860 --> 00:16:24,080
What is this? Where am I?

188
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:26,670
Am I dreaming right now?

189
00:16:26,670 --> 00:16:29,830
I even thought that.

190
00:16:29,830 --> 00:16:43,380
Because I hadn’t looked up this trekking place on Google beforehand... I just went there by chance.

191
00:16:43,380 --> 00:16:46,420
Imagine this, everyone.

192
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:56,530
You enter a forest you’ve never seen before by chance, and suddenly you come across this scene while walking.

193
00:16:56,530 --> 00:17:01,340
Isn’t it like a scene from a fairy tale?

194
00:17:01,340 --> 00:17:07,610
So I turned on Google Maps right there.

195
00:17:07,610 --> 00:17:09,620
I turned on Google Maps.

196
00:17:09,620 --> 00:17:11,030
Creek.

197
00:17:11,030 --> 00:17:13,140
It said “creek.”

198
00:17:13,140 --> 00:17:14,490
What is this?

199
00:17:14,490 --> 00:17:18,880
I didn’t know what that word was in English.

200
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:26,350
So I looked it up in Korean, but I didn’t know that either.

201
00:17:26,350 --> 00:17:31,150
I don't really understand Korean well either.

202
00:17:31,150 --> 00:17:32,470
Are you stupid?

203
00:17:32,470 --> 00:17:36,470
In Korean, it's called '만 (man)' which means 'creek.'

204
00:17:36,470 --> 00:17:37,760
Man

205
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:44,790
I've heard the word 'man' before, but it's not very familiar to me.

206
00:17:44,790 --> 00:17:50,890
It's so unfamiliar that it feels a bit awkward.

207
00:17:50,890 --> 00:17:56,500
In fact, it's a word that is rarely used.

208
00:17:56,500 --> 00:18:10,390
I'm familiar with words like lake, river, sea in Korean, but honestly, this 'creek' or 'man' is the first time I've seen it.

209
00:18:10,390 --> 00:18:15,290
There aren't many places called 'man' in Korea.

210
00:18:15,290 --> 00:18:21,240
So this word also feels a bit awkward.

211
00:18:21,240 --> 00:18:23,190
Isn't that interesting?

212
00:18:23,190 --> 00:18:32,750
While preparing this podcast, I looked for my diary again.

213
00:18:32,750 --> 00:18:42,140
Last time, when I did a soccer podcast, I showed my old diary.

214
00:18:42,140 --> 00:18:46,800
This time, it's an internet diary.

215
00:18:46,800 --> 00:18:48,390
A blog.

216
00:18:48,390 --> 00:18:53,240
I used to blog a bit in the past.

217
00:18:53,240 --> 00:19:01,450
But after trekking here, I wrote a short diary entry.

218
00:19:01,450 --> 00:19:12,750
A place with a beautiful blue gradient like someone splattered watercolor paint!

219
00:19:12,750 --> 00:19:15,130
Why do I feel so embarrassed?

220
00:19:15,130 --> 00:19:20,430
It's something I wrote, but it feels kind of embarrassing.

221
00:19:20,430 --> 00:19:23,920
Here is like heaven.

222
00:19:23,920 --> 00:19:26,410
There's a sentence like that.

223
00:19:26,410 --> 00:19:27,740
Heaven.

224
00:19:27,740 --> 00:19:30,970
I must have really liked it.

225
00:19:30,970 --> 00:19:37,050
I felt like I was Moana.

226
00:19:37,050 --> 00:19:40,570
Ah, why did I write like that? (lol)

227
00:19:40,570 --> 00:19:42,840
It's a bit cringy, right?

228
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:44,600
It's cringy.

229
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:52,040
Koreans make this hand gesture when something is cringy.

230
00:19:52,040 --> 00:19:53,490
Like this.

231
00:19:53,490 --> 00:20:00,680
Anyway, after trekking here, I really liked it.

232
00:20:00,680 --> 00:20:08,940
So I brought an older sister I know here.

233
00:20:08,940 --> 00:20:10,970
I brought her.

234
00:20:10,970 --> 00:20:15,480
I recommended this place to her.

235
00:20:15,480 --> 00:20:24,400
I said, "I found a really pretty place, do you want to go together?"

236
00:20:24,400 --> 00:20:29,370
I took her to the exact same place.

237
00:20:29,370 --> 00:20:31,000
I took her.

238
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:36,150
Excitedly, I said, "Isn't this place really pretty?"

239
00:20:36,150 --> 00:20:43,590
The air is so fresh, the sea is cool, it's the best! But...

240
00:20:43,590 --> 00:20:52,230
She said, "Is it really that much?"

241
00:20:52,230 --> 00:20:54,150
I'll be honest.

242
00:20:54,150 --> 00:21:00,080
She said, "It seems similar to Jeju Island."

243
00:21:00,080 --> 00:21:05,170
Jeju Island is a large island in Korea.

244
00:21:05,170 --> 00:21:06,420
Island.

245
00:21:06,420 --> 00:21:15,540
I've actually been to Jeju Island, but I haven't tried trekking there.

246
00:21:15,540 --> 00:21:21,080
Have you ever trekked in Jeju Island?

247
00:21:21,080 --> 00:21:24,500
It's called Olle-gil.

248
00:21:24,500 --> 00:21:26,240
Olle-gil.

249
00:21:26,240 --> 00:21:33,690
I definitely want to try trekking on Olle-gil someday.

250
00:21:33,690 --> 00:21:35,370
I will!

251
00:21:35,370 --> 00:21:40,570
Recently, hiking has become quite popular in Korea.

252
00:21:40,570 --> 00:21:49,370
Originally, hiking had an image of being liked by older people.

253
00:21:49,370 --> 00:21:56,880
Like the elderly wearing flashy hiking clothes, as I mentioned earlier.

254
00:21:56,880 --> 00:21:59,740
That was the image.

255
00:21:59,740 --> 00:22:06,830
But nowadays, young people also hike a lot.

256
00:22:06,830 --> 00:22:11,000
It's kind of like a hiking trend.

257
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:20,520
Among my Korean friends, there's one who quickly follows trends.

258
00:22:20,520 --> 00:22:30,070
One day, that friend posted a hiking proof photo on social media.

259
00:22:30,070 --> 00:22:32,740
What is a proof photo?

260
00:22:32,740 --> 00:22:34,900
It's a photo showing that you did something!

261
00:22:34,900 --> 00:22:39,700
A photo that proves you did something.

262
00:22:39,700 --> 00:22:42,230
It's like a challenge.

263
00:22:42,230 --> 00:22:48,180
For example, if you go to a book club.

264
00:22:48,180 --> 00:22:51,860
That's called a reading club.

265
00:22:51,860 --> 00:22:56,920
Then, like a challenge in that club,

266
00:22:56,920 --> 00:23:03,180
"Shall we send proof photos of what book we read this week?"

267
00:23:03,180 --> 00:23:06,780
People say things like this.

268
00:23:06,780 --> 00:23:16,980
When I used to teach group classes, I used the word 'proof photo' in Korean.

269
00:23:16,980 --> 00:23:28,920
I was in Korea, and the students lived in other countries, so I thought sending these proof photos to each other would be fun.

270
00:23:28,920 --> 00:23:39,010
For example, "Today's exercise" or "Shall we send proof photos of today's Korean study?" I said.

271
00:23:39,010 --> 00:23:44,230
But none of the students understood this word.

272
00:23:44,230 --> 00:23:45,490
No one.

273
00:23:45,490 --> 00:23:53,400
So I really wanted to tell you all about this in this video.

274
00:23:53,400 --> 00:23:59,560
It's a word used a lot among close people, a trendy word.

275
00:23:59,560 --> 00:24:08,120
When you exercise or read books together, you prove it.

276
00:24:08,120 --> 00:24:10,040
Proof photo.

277
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:13,720
That's called a proof shot (인증샷).

278
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:21,760
Taking and showing it to each other gives more motivation.

279
00:24:21,760 --> 00:24:23,860
It motivates you.

280
00:24:23,860 --> 00:24:39,480
So on Korean social media, taking a proof photo at the top of the mountain after hiking became popular.

281
00:24:39,480 --> 00:24:44,180
My friend always posts proof photos.

282
00:24:44,180 --> 00:24:49,390
I think these proof photos are good for motivation.

283
00:24:49,390 --> 00:24:53,810
Climbing a mountain is hard.

284
00:24:53,810 --> 00:25:00,410
But the good feeling you get when you reach the top!

285
00:25:00,410 --> 00:25:04,900
That refreshing and fulfilling feeling.

286
00:25:04,900 --> 00:25:13,600
The proud feeling when you endured all the hardships and climbed up.

287
00:25:13,600 --> 00:25:20,730
In cool Korean, this is called '성취감' (sense of accomplishment).

288
00:25:20,730 --> 00:25:22,000
Achievement.

289
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:26,710
Achievement means you accomplished something.

290
00:25:26,710 --> 00:25:28,250
I did it.

291
00:25:28,250 --> 00:25:29,490
I did it.

292
00:25:29,490 --> 00:25:31,160
It's me after all.

293
00:25:31,160 --> 00:25:33,050
Good job, myself!

294
00:25:33,050 --> 00:25:37,840
This feeling is called '성취감' (sense of accomplishment).

295
00:25:37,840 --> 00:25:49,130
So when you take proof photos and look at them later, you remember that sense of accomplishment.

296
00:25:49,130 --> 00:25:56,760
You should also take lots of proof photos when studying Korean.

297
00:25:56,760 --> 00:26:05,910
Sometimes students post Korean study proof photos on Instagram.

298
00:26:05,910 --> 00:26:10,840
Studying while watching my videos.

299
00:26:10,840 --> 00:26:20,310
Last week? The week before last, there was someone, and that looked so pretty.

300
00:26:20,310 --> 00:26:22,600
Why is it so pretty?

301
00:26:22,600 --> 00:26:28,420
I think the way you study Korean is so beautiful.

302
00:26:28,420 --> 00:26:38,300
Anyway, I hope you also feel a sense of accomplishment by taking these proof photos.

303
00:26:38,300 --> 00:26:48,330
When you feel tired and exhausted, when you want to give up, look at the proof photos you took before.

304
00:26:48,330 --> 00:26:57,380
Say, "Ah, I worked so hard like this!" and get your strength back. Fighting!

305
00:26:57,380 --> 00:27:03,410
I also take a lot of photos.

306
00:27:03,410 --> 00:27:09,500
I take a lot of reading proof photos, exercise proof photos, things like that.

307
00:27:09,500 --> 00:27:15,190
I have a social media account that I use just for myself.

308
00:27:15,190 --> 00:27:22,020
Shall I tell you something really funny and a bit ridiculous?

309
00:27:22,020 --> 00:27:27,800
I used to take vitamin proof photos in the past.

310
00:27:27,800 --> 00:27:32,660
Taking photos to prove I took medicine.

311
00:27:32,660 --> 00:27:34,100
Medicine photos.

312
00:27:34,100 --> 00:27:35,580
Huh?

313
00:27:35,580 --> 00:27:39,540
I really take all kinds of photos, right?

314
00:27:39,540 --> 00:27:43,990
Nowadays, I don't take medicine photos.

315
00:27:43,990 --> 00:27:56,430
Taking vitamin photos was a bit much! Too pointless! So I don't take them anymore.

316
00:27:56,430 --> 00:28:01,050
Today, we will read Korean news.

317
00:28:01,050 --> 00:28:06,450
Since it's news, some words might be a bit difficult.

318
00:28:06,450 --> 00:28:19,380
But reading news articles is really good for studying a foreign language, so let's look at it together.

319
00:28:19,380 --> 00:28:20,980
With me!

320
00:28:20,980 --> 00:28:22,330
Title.

321
00:28:22,330 --> 00:28:32,370
'Busy from weekend morning, MZ generation lining up to climb the mountain... What's the reason?'

322
00:28:32,370 --> 00:28:34,870
It says like this.

323
00:28:34,870 --> 00:28:36,740
This is the title.

324
00:28:36,740 --> 00:28:45,100
Do you remember the expression '북적북적' (bustling) we learned last time?

325
00:28:45,100 --> 00:28:50,450
If you don't remember, please watch the video again.

326
00:28:50,450 --> 00:29:02,090
This article talks about why young people in Korea like hiking these days.

327
00:29:02,090 --> 00:29:14,450
It says, 'Among the younger generation, as the SNS proof culture spreads.'

328
00:29:14,450 --> 00:29:16,270
I mentioned this earlier, right?

329
00:29:16,270 --> 00:29:17,680
Proof.

330
00:29:17,680 --> 00:29:23,840
Proof shot! Taking proof shots is a trend.

331
00:29:23,840 --> 00:29:32,650
Here, '확산되다' means the trend becomes more and more popular.

332
00:29:32,650 --> 00:29:35,130
It's a bit difficult word.

333
00:29:35,130 --> 00:29:38,500
확산되다 - to spread.

334
00:29:38,500 --> 00:29:42,730
Now, what shall we talk about next?

335
00:29:42,730 --> 00:29:52,720
Actually, when I think of Korean mountains, one story immediately comes to mind.

336
00:29:52,720 --> 00:29:58,910
This story is somewhat related to North Korea!

337
00:29:58,910 --> 00:30:00,190
North Korea.

338
00:30:00,190 --> 00:30:04,430
Mountains of North Korea and South Korea.

339
00:30:04,430 --> 00:30:07,800
There's a really interesting story...

340
00:30:07,800 --> 00:30:10,730
Shall we do it today?

341
00:30:10,730 --> 00:30:12,980
Or later?

342
00:30:12,980 --> 00:30:18,780
I don't want the video to get too long.

343
00:30:18,780 --> 00:30:25,810
Hmm, I'm wondering whether to do it now or later.

344
00:30:25,810 --> 00:30:31,510
Do you know what this kind of thing is called in Korean?

345
00:30:31,510 --> 00:30:33,040
Mil-dang.

346
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:36,790
There's a slang called '밀당' (mil-dang).

347
00:30:36,790 --> 00:30:45,200
Koreans pronounce the '당' part a bit strongly here.

348
00:30:45,200 --> 00:30:49,960
It's called '밀당' or '밀당하다.'

349
00:30:49,960 --> 00:30:54,650
It's a shortened form of '밀고 당기다' (push and pull).

350
00:30:54,650 --> 00:30:57,000
Like pushing.

351
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:01,150
What is pulling then?

352
00:31:01,150 --> 00:31:05,040
Pulling back what was pushed.

353
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:16,360
Like what I just did, "Hmm... Should we not talk about the mountain in North Korea today?" pushing it away.

354
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:21,620
Then suddenly, "Ah, should we talk about it now?" pulling it back.

355
00:31:21,620 --> 00:31:25,270
This is called '밀당.'

356
00:31:25,270 --> 00:31:31,150
This word is mostly used in romantic relationships.

357
00:31:31,150 --> 00:31:34,930
Boyfriend, girlfriend, romantic relationships.

358
00:31:34,930 --> 00:31:47,730
Someone likes the other person but feels it's not right to show it too much, so they pretend not to like and push them away.

359
00:31:47,730 --> 00:31:52,660
For example, someone asks me.

360
00:31:52,660 --> 00:31:56,630
"Ria, are you free this week?"

361
00:31:56,630 --> 00:32:01,160
Actually, I have a lot of free time, but just...

362
00:32:01,160 --> 00:32:05,430
Hmm, I don't think I can this week.

363
00:32:05,430 --> 00:32:07,170
Pretending not interested.

364
00:32:07,170 --> 00:32:09,860
That's pushing.

365
00:32:09,860 --> 00:32:16,980
Then suddenly! "I want to see you," expressing it.

366
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:22,330
Saying "I want to see you," pulling the person.

367
00:32:22,330 --> 00:32:25,010
This is called '밀당.'

368
00:32:25,010 --> 00:32:33,260
Many people do this push and pull, and many people dislike it too.

369
00:32:33,260 --> 00:32:35,380
What about you?

370
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:41,230
Later, I'll tell you a lot about Korean dating stories.

371
00:32:41,230 --> 00:32:46,480
If you have any questions, please leave comments in advance.

372
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:52,340
Then I'll make videos based on those questions.

373
00:32:52,340 --> 00:33:04,840
Anyway, there's an interesting story about mountains in North and South Korea, and I'll definitely tell you later.

374
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:10,070
I won't forget, I'll make a note.

375
00:33:10,070 --> 00:33:17,220
Wow, today I did a podcast about mountains for the first time.

376
00:33:17,220 --> 00:33:23,220
But honestly, I still have so much I want to say.

377
00:33:23,220 --> 00:33:29,060
I want to talk about Korean mountains that foreigners like,

378
00:33:29,060 --> 00:33:35,350
and as I said earlier, I want to talk about mountains in North Korea.

379
00:33:35,350 --> 00:33:46,240
I have so much to tell you in the future, so I hope you listen to my podcast a lot.

380
00:33:46,240 --> 00:33:53,200
Then, let's meet next time with a more fun podcast.

381
00:33:53,200 --> 00:34:00,960
If you want to study more fun with me today, please click this link!

382
00:34:00,960 --> 00:34:03,990
Then have a nice day today!

383
00:34:03,990 --> 00:34:07,120
See you next time, bye! :)
